course: dictionary
lesson: 6098
title: Keeping the beat with a metronome
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A metronome is a device that ticks or clicks at a steady speed to help musicians keep time. Keeping time means playing each note at the right moment, so the beat stays even.
explain: Read the context: «A metronome is a ticking machine or app that keeps a steady beat; groups often rush when they get excited.» The clue is steady beat. A steady beat stays the same speed all the way through.
explain: The word is also used for anything very regular, like «ticking like a metronome». If you hear someone described that way, picture an even, steady tick and you will understand.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The band used a metronome to stop them rushing» still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A metronome is a device that ticks or clicks at a steady speed.
fact: Musicians use a metronome to help them keep time.
fact: «A metronome is a ticking machine or app that keeps a steady beat.»
fact: «Groups often rush when they get excited» means they start playing faster than the steady beat.
fact: Steady means even and not changing, so «ticking like a metronome» describes something very regular.
exercise: What does a metronome do? | *It ticks at a steady speed to help musicians keep time | It tells you the weather | It makes music louder | It mends broken instruments
exercise: «Groups often rush when they get excited.» What does rush mean in music? | Stop playing completely | Play very quietly | *Play faster than the steady beat | Sing a brand new song
exercise: Which word in «a ticking machine or app that keeps a steady beat» tells you the beat stays the same? | *steady | noisy | machine | app
test: A band wants to keep the same speed all the way through a song. What helps them most? | A torch | *A metronome | A loud drum kit that changes speed | A bag of sweets
test: «Her heartbeat was ticking like a metronome.» What does that suggest? | Lost in the dark | *Regular and steady | Fast, messy and uneven | Very loud and angry
exam: Which sentence uses metronome correctly? | The metronome was a tasty cake at the party | The metronome was a fast and wild dog | The metronome flew over the hills | *The metronome ticked at a steady speed as the pianist practised
